WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:02.252
On November 8th, the US will hold 

00:00:02.252 --> 00:00:05.797
475 separate federal elections.

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:09.050
If you vote in the US, you can vote 
in at least one of them.

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:11.845
The results will determine 
who controls these:

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:13.680
the two houses of Congress.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.559
After the 2020 election, Democrats
controlled the House of Representatives

00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:20.103
and the Senate, as well as 
the Presidency.

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:22.355
The three bodies 
that have to align

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:24.607
if you want to make 
new laws.

00:00:24.607 --> 00:00:25.483
And they did.

00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:27.986
But even when government is split 
between the parties.

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:30.280
Each one of these bodies 
still holds

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.615
tremendous power
on its own.

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:35.326
So the importance of what 
this picture looks like

00:00:35.326 --> 00:00:36.911
can't be overstated.

00:00:36.911 --> 00:00:39.372
And each one of these 
three outcomes

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:42.375
contains a very different story

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.586
about the next 2 years in the US.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:49.174
Scenario 1: Democrats
keep both houses.

00:00:49.758 --> 00:00:51.801
This is not considered 
the most likely outcome.

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:55.013
The Democrats have gotten a ton passed
in the last two years.

00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:56.097
My name's Li Zhou.

00:00:56.097 --> 00:00:58.016
I am a politics reporter 
at Vox.

00:00:58.016 --> 00:01:00.435
I've been covering the hill for 
over 6 years now.

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:04.439
So if Democrats get to stick with 
this arrangement...  then what?

00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:06.858
One of the big things that
they've said they'll do 

00:01:06.858 --> 00:01:09.694
is codify Roe V. Wade 
into federal law,

00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:10.820
and that would mean

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.199
a national protection for 
access to abortion.

00:01:14.199 --> 00:01:17.035
Paid family leave, subsidies
for child care,

00:01:17.035 --> 00:01:20.789
universal pre-K, the PRO act which 
protects people's ability 

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:22.040
to organize and unionize.

00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:24.125
But there's a catch 
to all this.

00:01:24.125 --> 00:01:28.296
And it's connected to the reason Democrats 
haven't done any of it yet.

00:01:28.296 --> 00:01:30.757
These laws are subject to a rule 
the Senate has

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:31.800
(It's called the filibuster)

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.928
that requires not 51 out of 100 
votes to pass a law...

00:01:35.303 --> 00:01:37.388
but 60 out of 100.

00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:38.807
And Democrats want to 
change that rule.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:41.684
Just... not all Democrats.

00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:44.562
They have this 50/50
very narrow majority

00:01:44.562 --> 00:01:48.358
but they only have 48 who are actually 
down to change the rules.

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:50.652
And so they need at least 52,

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:52.195
is kind of the magic number

00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:54.322
 that Democrats have been 
hoping for, in order to both

00:01:54.322 --> 00:01:56.908
change the rules, and then 
pass a lot of the bills

00:01:56.908 --> 00:01:58.243
that we've talked about.

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:03.456
Scenario 2: Republicans win 
the House of Representatives. 

00:02:03.665 --> 00:02:06.084
When the House is controlled
by a different party 

00:02:06.084 --> 00:02:07.377
than the presidency 
or the Senate.

00:02:07.377 --> 00:02:08.962
That gives them a lot of leverage.

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:09.879
My name's Dylan Matthews.

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:11.131
I'm a reporter at Vox.

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:15.009
They also have a lot of control 
over investigations.

00:02:15.051 --> 00:02:16.010
They can run committees.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:17.011
They can subpoena people.

00:02:17.011 --> 00:02:19.389
They can make people testify. 
They can dredge up documents.

00:02:19.681 --> 00:02:21.224
We can see that by going back

00:02:21.224 --> 00:02:23.101
to the last time the government 
looked like this.

00:02:23.101 --> 00:02:25.395
One thing that the GOP majority

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:27.564
in 2011 and onward did 
was investigate

00:02:27.564 --> 00:02:29.315
the Obama administration extensively.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:32.402
The "fast and furious" 
gun smuggling scandal,

00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:36.739
on the Benghazi scandal after 
those attacks happened in 2012.

00:02:36.781 --> 00:02:38.533
"-Easily obtained...."
"Well, but Senator, again—"

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:39.868
"Within hours, if not days."

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:41.911
After Democrats won in 2018...

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:44.247
They also launched 
a bunch of investigations

00:02:44.247 --> 00:02:46.583
into the Trump administration,  one of which

00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:48.418
culminated in the first impeachment.

00:02:48.751 --> 00:02:50.753
Investigations can matter a lot.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:53.006
More often than not, 
they're kind of a sideshow.

00:02:54.215 --> 00:02:55.508
My sense ahead of time

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:59.387
is that that lever of power 
will be less important

00:02:59.387 --> 00:03:02.307
than the ability to block 
must-pass legislation

00:03:02.307 --> 00:03:03.850
on spending and 
the debt ceiling.

00:03:05.393 --> 00:03:06.769
The debt ceiling fight in 2011

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:09.689
was one of the first stories I covered 
at The Washington Post.

00:03:09.731 --> 00:03:10.857
The debt ceiling is this limit 

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:12.775
on how much the federal government 
can borrow.

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:15.403
Almost the entirety of the 
international financial system

00:03:15.403 --> 00:03:20.366
is built on the idea that 
US Treasury bonds are a safe asset.

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:23.828
Once you hit the debt ceiling,
they are no longer a low risk asset.

00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:26.414
That would lead to investments,

00:03:26.414 --> 00:03:29.459
and borrowing, and homes 
and everything

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:31.586
being way more expensive 
throughout the world

00:03:31.586 --> 00:03:34.380
which would be a pretty major 
financial crisis.

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:36.758
You need the House and Senate 
and the President 

00:03:36.758 --> 00:03:38.593
to agree for the debt
ceiling to be raised.

00:03:38.593 --> 00:03:40.220
The most dramatic example of that

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.388
happened in the summer of 2011.

00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:45.808
The debt ceiling was coming 
due in early August.

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:49.896
That gave House Speaker John Boehner 
and members of his caucus

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:52.649
incredible leverage. Unless 
the Obama administration

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:54.734
was willing to let the debt ceiling 
be breached,

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.319
they kind of had to come to a deal.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:00.615
Ultimately, $2.1 trillion in cuts

00:04:00.615 --> 00:04:03.993
to the National Parks, Head Start programs, 
the FBI

00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:06.579
National Institutes of Health,
the National Science Foundation.

00:04:06.871 --> 00:04:09.415
One area of the budget 
is pandemic prevention.

00:04:09.707 --> 00:04:12.710
The choice that was made 
to invest a lot less

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.046
in preventing pandemics 
as a part of this deal..

00:04:15.505 --> 00:04:16.798
It certainly couldn't have helped.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.678
I mostly expect this Congress
to be more hard-line,

00:04:21.678 --> 00:04:24.806
in terms of their demands and 
unwillingness to compromise.

00:04:26.724 --> 00:04:30.061
Scenario 3: Republicans win 
the House and the Senate.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:34.148
The Senate has to confirm 
any federal judge.

00:04:34.148 --> 00:04:36.276
I'm Ian Millhiser,
I'm a lawyer

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:38.820
and I cover the Supreme Court 
for Vox.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.363
The president nominates

00:04:40.363 --> 00:04:42.824
anyone who's going to be appointed 
to the federal bench

00:04:42.824 --> 00:04:44.158
but they don't get the job

00:04:44.158 --> 00:04:46.661
unless a majority of 
the Senate votes

00:04:46.661 --> 00:04:48.413
to confirm that individual.

00:04:48.413 --> 00:04:50.957
There's a little more than 800

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:53.501
active judges in the federal system.

00:04:53.668 --> 00:04:56.796
There are district judges 
who try cases,

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:59.173
Court of Appeals or 
circuit judges.

00:04:59.173 --> 00:05:00.967
And then there's 
the Supreme Court.

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:04.929
The last time we had a Democratic president 
and a Republican Senate

00:05:04.929 --> 00:05:07.140
was in 2015 and 2016.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.685
Under Barack Obama, 
the Republican Senate

00:05:10.685 --> 00:05:14.814
basically hit stop on 
Supreme Court confirmations

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:17.859
and on nearly all 
Court of Appeals confirmations.

00:05:18.026 --> 00:05:19.402
And then, of course,
what happened was

00:05:19.402 --> 00:05:21.654
Republicans held all those seats open

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.823
until Donald Trump 
got into office

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.950
and then filled them 
with Republicans.

00:05:26.868 --> 00:05:29.162
Trump's judges have 
overruled Roe v. Wade,

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:32.999
which is the reason why abortion 
is now illegal in many US states.

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:35.168
A sweeping reinterpretation

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:38.046
of the Second Amendment,
our firearms amendment.

00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:41.799
And now judges are striking down 
gun laws left and right.

00:05:41.799 --> 00:05:43.926
A wholesale attack on voting rights,

00:05:43.926 --> 00:05:48.222
particularly on the Voting Rights Act,
which is the law that prevents

00:05:48.222 --> 00:05:49.432
race discrimination.

00:05:49.432 --> 00:05:51.059
At the lower court level,

00:05:51.059 --> 00:05:55.032
we just had three Trump judges 
declare the entire

00:05:55.032 --> 00:05:58.524
Consumer Financial Protection
Bureau unconstitutional.

00:05:58.775 --> 00:06:00.318
If Republicans take the Senate

00:06:00.318 --> 00:06:04.009
then they can block the confirmation 
of any judge.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.074
If you hold those seats open,

00:06:06.074 --> 00:06:08.451
they're still vacant when a Republican
comes to office,

00:06:08.451 --> 00:06:10.370
and then the Republican 
can fill them.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:11.704
The Republican Party

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:14.957
which is an institution 
that wants power...

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.628
has figured out that if 
it controls the judiciary,

00:06:18.628 --> 00:06:22.882
it can gain, potentially, 
a permanent veto power 

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:24.842
over any law that's enacted

00:06:24.842 --> 00:06:27.553
over any regulatory policy
that's enacted

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:29.514
regardless of who controls 
the White House

00:06:29.514 --> 00:06:32.016
and regardless of who 
controls the Congress.

00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:34.143
They're going after 
voting rights hard.

00:06:34.143 --> 00:06:35.853
And if you don't have 
the right to vote

00:06:35.853 --> 00:06:38.314
then you don't have any rights.

00:06:38.314 --> 00:06:41.901
Eventually, the voters who've
been disenfranchized

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:43.277
don't have any recourse

00:06:43.277 --> 00:06:46.364
because they have no way to change
who controls the government.

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:49.784
And the government is controlled by people
who don't share their interests.

00:06:49.784 --> 00:06:51.452
So that's the worst case scenario.

00:06:51.452 --> 00:06:53.704
Right now, we're still 
at the point where

00:06:53.704 --> 00:06:57.417
you know, elections are the 
best method that can be used

00:06:57.417 --> 00:07:00.586
in order to reverse
America's democratic decline.

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:03.923
Out of the 475 individual

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:06.300
House and Senate elections
that will decide control of Congress...

00:07:06.843 --> 00:07:09.053
most of them are not close races.

00:07:09.887 --> 00:07:12.598
The election will really be decided 
by these races...

00:07:13.015 --> 00:07:15.268
only about 1 out of 7 
House elections

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:17.478
and just a handful of Senate races.

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:21.023
And so if you live in 
one of those places...

00:07:21.357 --> 00:07:28.350
it means you have a lot of power over 
which one of these we choose.

